## Application Availability

Don't create this using the Memory-style Custom Threshold rule if you're already using Elastic Synthetics. Use a **Monitor status/Synthetics rule**.

From your spreadsheet:

```text
Warning:
Rule name: SP - Application Availability - Warning
Evaluation window: 15 minutes
Check every: 5 minutes
Consecutive failures: 2

Critical:
Rule name: SP - Application Availability - Critical
Evaluation window: 15 minutes
Check every: 5 minutes
Consecutive failures: 3
```

**Subject**

```text
🚨 [Application Availability] {{context.monitorName}} is DOWN
```

Body:

```text
{{rule.name}}

Application availability alert

Monitor: {{context.monitorName}}
Status: {{context.status}}
URL: {{context.monitorUrl}}
Location: {{context.locationName}}

Reason:
{{context.reason}}

Error:
{{context.lastErrorMessage}}

Alert ID: {{alert.uuid}}
Rule ID: {{rule.id}}
```

## SQL Server Service

This is different from CPU/Memory because it normally depends on an event such as **service.state**, Windows Event Log, or a custom SQL-service field.

Your spreadsheet indicates roughly:

```text
Rule name:
SP - SQL Server Service Status - Warning

Purpose:
Detect when SQL Server service stops or becomes unavailable

Evaluation window:
5 minutes

Check every:
1 minute

Consecutive runs:
2
```

Critical:

```text
SP - SQL Server Service Status - Critical

Check every:
1 minute

Consecutive runs:
3
```

I would use an **Elasticsearch query rule** if the SQL service state is represented by log/event documents. Elasticsearch query rules can include matching document fields in the email through `context.hits`. ([Elastic][3])

Example subject:

```text
🚨 [SQL Service Critical] SQL Server service unavailable
```

Body:

```text
{{rule.name}}

SQL Server service availability alert

{{#context.hits}}
Host: {{_source.host.name}}
Service: {{_source.service.name}}
State: {{_source.service.state}}
Event time: {{_source.@timestamp}}
{{/context.hits}}

Alert ID: {{alert.uuid}}
Rule ID: {{rule.id}}
```

